re so if if your application can  run in a docker container then awesome  and if you start at the App Engine level  so if you're starting a new project and  you start at a very high level of  abstraction if you want to move down  it's a change one config line and you go  from App Engine standard to Flex once  you're in Flex it creates a docker image  every time you deploy so you can take  that same docker image and you can run  that in container engine or you can run  that in compute engine so you can take  the exact same code and run it in all of  these environments already if you're  starting a compute engine if you go up  like you may have to there's some work  involved in getting it into containers  and then you can do the same thing you  can kind of move up the stack and decide  where you want to run it also most  systems that are mature they end up  using multiple parts of things you might  have an app engine front-end for your  web api and some kind of long-running  processes maybe do transcoding video  transcoding  or some other work in VMs so it's very  common to have a system that's made up  of using these together okay so back to  the very beginning if you've got an  event-driven problem start at cloud  functions if you are starting a new  thing you know you're going to be on the  web  start with App Engine if you're all-in  on containers kubernetes container  engine and if you have an existing  system you should probably start looking  at compute engine and that is the core  of that and I would like to hand over to  actually and this clicker for you  thank you thank you  move autos as you could understand I  could have done this presentation in  French but I want Brian to understand it  so I will do it in English as well so my  name is Eddie Mira I am one of the  co-founders and CTU of few Maps and I'm  really glad to be here today to tell you  more about our journey using Google  cloud platform it's been more than five  years now so first what what is lu maps  so lu maps is the omni cooperate and  social portal recommended by Google and  it's fully integrated with Google G  suite so it's basically everything you  need to work in one place and our goal  is to make sure that you will never need  your old intranet again so Lu Maps is a  social mobile and smart intranet and to  connect and engage employees it brings  all your internal communication in just  a central hub so corporate news business  applications including G suite and  social collaboration it's built on top  of G suite and it integrates with the  directory with groups with Drive with  Gmail with calendar with sites and much  more and we are really proud at  Maps to have a team one Android person  dedicated to the Google cloud platform  and you know being a recommended Google  partner means that we work Google and  loom apps together to align all the  product roadmaps and as you can see our  solution has been adapted already by  major G suite customers so for instance  Colgate Logitech and also as I've  recently joined the loom apps family so  just to give you a bit more information  about our story so we created the  company in 2012 so at the beginning it  was just three guys we were not in a  garage because in France people work in  an office you know and we we studied at  the time where when the Google cloud  platform did not even exist so at that  time it was called App Engine and it was  a very very new product but you know  when we started we didn't have the  necessary financial resources and all  the time not the people to rely on our  own infrastructure and we would just  want it to build our product and that's  why we chose App Engine which was at the  time the only fully managed platform  available so we started a company with a  Java stack but we quickly move to Python  because we loved the simplicity of using  it and we really like Google  technologies at new maps so we adopted  angularjs at the same time and we built  an open-source framework which is called  Lumix  it's like material design for angularjs  it's open source so you should check it  out and you know since then more and  more companies have been using our  product our team has been growing our  App Engine invoice also has been growing  bigger months after months but that's  good news and you know when Google  recommended us at the end of last year  or when a big customer like via Vallejo  with 50,000 employees went live we  should have been scared will it work  will the platform run under such a load  the answer is yes and you know  we add like the most boring go live ever  so we were just in front of our  computers looking at the platform going  to explode and it just works and and you  can go live on a huge number of people  and and the platform will work so as you  can see we've been relying on almost  only the standard app engine from the  beginning and we've been adding some  pieces along the way so our bacon is in  pison and we use all the niceties from  Google App Engine so we use the cache  system we use the native integration  with cloud datastore which is a no  sequel database and we use the queuing  system you know to endow a large task  that you want to - - to run in the in  the background we also use Cloud  endpoints which is a framework to build  some API so you have all the O's -  authentication built in it's the same  technology that runs the API on all the  Google product so drive gmail calendar  and so on and it's used by all our  clients - the web Android iOS and all  our customers while doing some you know  custom integration using the API so my  diagram here is not totally accurate  because we have some pcs running outside  of App Engine so for instance we have a  small cluster of elasticsearch I will  explain after why we have this and we  also have some machine learning pieces  which are running on compute engine and  with this you know we can interact with  external services so that can be all the  drive API Calendar API Gmail API as well  as other third parties  we also plan on integrating with  firebase for real-time into notification  and directions in the product you know  so you can do pretty much everything  with App Engine but sometimes when you  use it you will it like a roadblock and  you you have to ask this question should  I move away from App Engine should I go  down the stack add to container engine  or compute engine  well is this happened to us at various  occasions and we always had to come up  with like a pragmatic solution to handle  these situations so let me tell you the  story of our search engine for instance  so we use it's built on top of the App  Engine Search API and some surgeries API  so the App Engine Search API is really  great  it allows you to index documents and do  complex queries at scale it's fully  managed and and don't get me wrong it  just worked so for us it was like the  perfect search system so we started  using it in production back in 2012 and  at this time it was like an alpha  product so it was very very new but it  has always worked for us except when you  do some very complex queries which  exceed two thousand bytes and when you  do that it just doesn't work and so this  is the limit but we needed more because  we have some very complex queries to run  but this is a very small fraction of our  queries so the question was should we  remove the Search API for those queries  and the answer was no we will just  create a small elastic search cluster  and index the documents twice and for  the small fraction of queries that do  not work with the Search API we will use  the elastic shots cluster instead and  this cluster run on compute engine and  is the same with the API  the Cloud endpoints framework is really  great to expose your API and have a way  for people to interact with it online  but it doesn't come with a good  documentation system yet so we had to  act a way into creating such a portal  but as the framework for Cloud endpoints  open source we were able to to just  create this portal using this open  source libraries and so just a few lips  tips if you if you want to be using App  Engine efficiently the first thing that  you should read the manual there are  some very good stuff in the  documentation from from Google  especially the English version which is  more up-to-date you shouldn't be afraid  to use services that are really really  new because they work so we use some  service in alpha mode and it was just ok  you should subscribe to the mailing list  from App Engine because there is some  really good stuff here and you can get  some some good information and the last  point is not obvious but you should pay  for support because sometimes when you  are in production you will have some  issues and you want the support team to  give you some answers very quickly so  everything is great great with App  Engine but we have a few challenges  today so the first one is about  measuring and debugging so App Engine  comes with a stack driver which a lot of  features to do some some logging some  analytics on the codes on debugging so  it's really great it's really good to  have access to all the logging system  and to be able to to search inside the  logs but sometimes you need more you  need to have an agent installed on the  most on the machine and you cannot you  can't do this with with standard so for  instance if you want to use a tool like  New Relic or that  you won't be able to do it with develop  and just stand down and you will have to  move to to flex then we have some I  wouldn't say issues but sometime it can  be tricky to have your application  working in in some countries especially  in China where App Engine is not like  the the best tool over in this countries  you have some some quotas also so for  instance if you intend to run to send a  lot a lot very large number of emails  you will be limited on App Engine  because they want to prevent you know  spam and so on so keep in mind those  those quotas and if you need more you  should consider using an Excel service  instead for the database so you have  cloud datastore which is really a good  tool but you you need to know how to use  it because if you use it as a sequel  database you it will just not work so  keep in mind that it's designed for a  scale and that there is some trade-off  when you design your data model and if  you do that you can really have  something which with which will scale  with no limit and the last point is  about moving from standard to flags  so as Brian was saying if you want to to  move from one to another you need to  read the couple the services that you  are using because natively on standard  you can use the cache system the cue  system and if you and the data store and  you you cannot do this directly using  the same libraries on flex so you you  know you there are some trade-off on how  you can move from from one to to the  other and you know finally let's talk  about the new stuff that we are walking  on so the first one is cloud spanner and  this one is really exciting  it offers the scalability of a managed  database but with the flexibility of  sequel so we will implement it for  complex objects with relationships where  the cloud data store is maybe too  limited for for this it's not the right  tool we also want to implement a  multiple multi region infrastructure  because we have instances in multiple  regions in App Engine and natively you  there is no like a load balancer on top  of all these regions you have to do it  on your own and so we are looking at the  best way to aggregate all of the  instances under same infrastructure and  finally so our dream a tree Maps is to  build a personal assistant for employees  and to be proactive to serve the right  information to the right people at the  right time and to do that we've studied  implementing machine learning algorithms  and we want to intensify the usage and  build on top of cloud ml to scale our  models and refine our data and so  finally when I prepare for this  presentation Brian asked me a question  he said if you had to make the decision  today would you make the same decision  and the answer is yes you know App  Engine has been really Apfel along the  way and frankly I'm not sure I would be  here today if we had not make this  choice five years ago so today we serve  1.5 million users with only one person  doing some DevOps in the team so it's  you know we we were able to really focus  on building the product which is great  what we could have done better is the  decoupling between standard appengine  features to make sure that it's easy to  switch from one system to another from  standard to flex to container engine and  to compute engine that's all thank you  you  [Applause]
 

